# Shrinking with whichever codec here wins on the tree at hand.
#
# Only the deferred form can choose per tree, which is why there is no
# [encode_best] beside it: [Codec.shrink]'s result carries the decode it needs,
# so nothing downstream has to know which codec made it, where a payload on its
# own does not say.
#
# It costs both encodings, and the term codec's is mostly the check that its
# candidate gives the tree back, so this is for a tree whose size is worth
# paying that once at build time.

shrink_best = \t Codec.smaller
  (Codec.shrink Codec.Grammar.codec t)
  (Codec.shrink Codec.Term.codec t)

# Which one wins says something about the tree. The grammar codec wins
# wherever a tree is merely repetitive, and by a wide margin on a large one.
# The term codec wins where a program was written with it in mind — binding
# what its inner code uses close to it — and the selfie is the one such program
# here; its own tests keep that figure, since depending on it from this module
# would be a cycle.
_to_string = \l List.join " → " $ List.map Nat.to_string l
[size Lambada.parse_expr; size (shrink_best Lambada.parse_expr)]
# = 491776 → 4812
# Below what either encoding costs to carry, the tree comes back as it was.
[size Bool.not; size (shrink_best Bool.not)]
# = 8 → 8

_to_string = Bool.to_source
equal (shrink_best List.map Nat.succ [1; 2]) [2; 3]
# = true
